"The postoperative pathology reveals a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) diagnosis with pathological stage pT3a, demonstrating extraprostatic extension and positive surgical margins. A reassuring finding is that the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. Nodal status is uncertain as no lymph nodes were removed, meaning pNx does not establish node-negative disease. The preoperative PSA was 8.3. The documented findings of positive margins and T3a extension are adverse prognostic factors. Conversely, the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymphovascular invasion are reassuring. The biopsy showed a Gleason 3+3 diagnosis in the right peripheral zone. Postoperative PSA is not recorded, which prevents assessment of biochemical recurrence. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory is crucial for revising the prognosis. Missing nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding the extent of disease. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.42 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 54.87–113.97 months; its coverage has not been validated."
